Now I can see the issue! The commit `55ad5d7bd7` introduced `assertExplicitProxySupportsPinnedDns` which throws an error for plain HTTP targets:

```typescript
function assertExplicitProxySupportsPinnedDns(
  url: URL,
  dispatcherPolicy?: PinnedDispatcherPolicy,
  pinDns?: boolean,
): void {
  if (
    pinDns !== false &&
    dispatcherPolicy?.mode === "explicit-proxy" &&
    url.protocol !== "https:"
  ) {
    throw new Error(
      "Explicit proxy SSRF pinning requires HTTPS targets; plain HTTP targets are not supported",
    );
  }
}
```

The Telegram file URLs are at `https://api.telegram.org/file/...` - they should be HTTPS. But the issue mentions `api.telegram.org/file/...` without specifying the protocol, so it could be an HTTP URL or the code is constructing URLs that use HTTP.


Let me check how the Telegram media fetch code is constructing these URLs.

